WEBVTT

00:00:00.103 --> 00:00:03.922
How can we choose a revenue cap from an experiment and still trust

00:00:03.802 --> 00:00:05.094
the test that follows

00:00:05.674 --> 00:00:09.866
Start with the problem A few big spenders can dominate an experiment

00:00:10.424 --> 00:00:15.170
In this toy example treatment changes nobody's revenue Nine people

00:00:15.050 --> 00:00:17.966
spend ten dollars and one spends two hundred

00:00:18.561 --> 00:00:22.032
Whichever group receives that big spender has an average of forty

00:00:21.912 --> 00:00:27.045
eight dollars The other group averages ten Random assignment alone

00:00:27.015 --> 00:00:28.282
creates the difference

00:00:29.311 --> 00:00:33.723
Capping limits each person's contribution Here a two hundred dollar

00:00:33.603 --> 00:00:38.400
observation is counted as fifty dollars reducing its influence

00:00:38.978 --> 00:00:43.557
But capping can also remove real growth If treatment raises a person's

00:00:43.553 --> 00:00:47.410
spending from two hundred to two hundred forty dollars a fifty dollar

00:00:47.290 --> 00:00:49.266
cap makes both outcomes fifty

00:00:49.866 --> 00:00:54.419
The question is which cap preserves useful signal while reducing noise

00:00:54.737 --> 00:00:58.066
Capped revenue is a different outcome from total revenue

00:00:59.090 --> 00:01:03.437
Our method starts with a belief fixed before random assignment For

00:01:03.317 --> 00:01:08.038
example suppose treatment increases everyone's spending by two percent

00:01:08.683 --> 00:01:13.133
For a control user who spends ten dollars the belief supplies a treated

00:01:13.026 --> 00:01:15.840
outcome of ten dollars and twenty cents

00:01:16.433 --> 00:01:20.458
For a treated user who spends twenty dollars it supplies a control

00:01:20.338 --> 00:01:23.886
outcome of about nineteen dollars and sixty one cents

00:01:24.478 --> 00:01:29.083
Solid cells are observed Outlined cells are imputed This completed

00:01:28.976 --> 00:01:33.941
table guides selection it does not reveal the true missing outcomes

00:01:34.978 --> 00:01:40.910
We enumerate candidate caps For each cap truncate both completed columns

00:01:40.983 --> 00:01:44.763
and calculate the predicted effect divided by its predicted standard

00:01:44.643 --> 00:01:45.150
error

00:01:45.728 --> 00:01:50.655
This is the belief's predicted signal to noise ratio Select the candidate

00:01:50.548 --> 00:01:51.866
with the largest score

00:01:52.424 --> 00:01:57.389
In this toy example that candidate is twenty dollars The paper's reported

00:01:57.282 --> 00:02:01.732
simulations compare six hundred candidates on a logarithmic grid

00:02:02.311 --> 00:02:06.942
This finds the best candidate under the belief-based score not necessarily

00:02:06.835 --> 00:02:08.127
the true best cap

00:02:09.186 --> 00:02:13.894
Once the cap is selected return to the actual treatment and control

00:02:13.774 --> 00:02:14.757
observations

00:02:15.353 --> 00:02:20.486
Apply the same cap to both groups The treatment mean minus the control

00:02:20.675 --> 00:02:22.651
mean estimates the capped effect

00:02:23.228 --> 00:02:26.815
Divide that difference by the standard error computed from the observed

00:02:26.695 --> 00:02:29.933
capped groups to obtain the final test statistic

00:02:30.507 --> 00:02:35.343
Imputed outcomes do not enter this final calculation The belief affects

00:02:35.236 --> 00:02:36.864
it through the selected cap

00:02:37.895 --> 00:02:41.468
These are two distinct calculations using the same experiment

00:02:42.036 --> 00:02:46.859
On the left observations and a fixed belief form the completed table

00:02:47.139 --> 00:02:51.473
In the middle grid search chooses the largest predicted signal to

00:02:51.353 --> 00:02:52.658
noise ratio

00:02:53.270 --> 00:02:57.823
On the right only the selected cap carries forward Actual observed

00:02:57.703 --> 00:02:59.936
incomes are capped and tested

00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:05.550
Because the observations influence both stages this is not automatically

00:03:05.442 --> 00:03:09.983
equivalent to choosing the cap before the experiment The theory must

00:03:09.875 --> 00:03:11.503
account for that dependence

00:03:12.561 --> 00:03:16.831
The two theorems answer different questions Theorem one bounds the

00:03:16.724 --> 00:03:21.779
probability of losing too much score relative to the best deterministic

00:03:21.672 --> 00:03:24.408
belief score The belief may be wrong

00:03:24.978 --> 00:03:29.054
This is a score guarantee not a guarantee of being close to the true

00:03:28.947 --> 00:03:33.732
optimal cap Multiple peaks are allowed With a separated best candidate

00:03:33.999 --> 00:03:38.294
a further bound compares rejection probabilities with a fixed belief-best

00:03:38.187 --> 00:03:38.706
cap

00:03:39.308 --> 00:03:44.145
Theorem two concerns false positives With quantitative localization

00:03:44.270 --> 00:03:48.410
variance bounds and nonpositive capped effects in the local region

00:03:48.690 --> 00:03:52.753
the limiting upper rejection probability is at most the nominal level

00:03:53.353 --> 00:03:58.306
Distant near-ties fall outside this localization guarantee though

00:03:58.186 --> 00:04:03.847
inflation is not inevitable A single peak in a picture does not verify

00:04:03.740 --> 00:04:08.550
the assumptions Interpreting the result for total revenue additionally

00:04:08.611 --> 00:04:11.295
needs the paper's sign-preservation assumption

00:04:12.311 --> 00:04:16.826
Now consider a simulation where treatment affects nobody Every positive

00:04:16.706 --> 00:04:18.797
detection is a false positive

00:04:19.353 --> 00:04:24.035
The orange method tries every cap using the actual group comparison

00:04:24.327 --> 00:04:28.661
and chooses the largest observed test statistic Its false-positive

00:04:28.541 --> 00:04:31.458
rate is about thirty to thirty four percent

00:04:32.061 --> 00:04:35.661
The green method selects using the fixed belief's predicted signal

00:04:35.618 --> 00:04:40.841
to noise ratio then tests the observed data Its rate is about ten

00:04:40.734 --> 00:04:42.748
percent in these simulations

00:04:43.353 --> 00:04:48.254
Both use the same nominal ten percent test threshold Importantly the

00:04:48.134 --> 00:04:52.983
green method's belief assumes two percent growth so that belief is

00:04:52.863 --> 00:04:55.303
wrong in this no-effect experiment

00:04:55.882 --> 00:05:00.628
Repeated random assignments estimate these actual false-positive rates

00:05:00.921 --> 00:05:04.984
These reported simulations have not been rerun for this presentation

00:05:05.994 --> 00:05:11.707
Next treatment really increases total revenue by two percent in all

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:15.882
three panels The same proportional belief is used throughout

00:05:16.465 --> 00:05:21.688
On the left everyone grows proportionally matching the belief In the

00:05:21.581 --> 00:05:26.173
middle a subset of payers receives a fixed increment On the right

00:05:26.607 --> 00:05:31.173
only heavy spenders grow Those two patterns do not match the belief

00:05:31.741 --> 00:05:36.784
The horizontal axis is sample size The vertical axis is the share

00:05:36.677 --> 00:05:40.650
of repeated experiments rejecting the null in the positive direction

00:05:40.955 --> 00:05:43.343
The blue curve is the belief method

00:05:43.936 --> 00:05:48.592
The original paper figure shows that equal total growth does not imply

00:05:48.588 --> 00:05:52.768
equal detectability What matters is where the growth occurs and what

00:05:52.648 --> 00:05:54.469
the selected cap preserves

00:05:55.520 --> 00:06:00.356
The rightmost case illustrates an important limitation Total revenue

00:06:00.249 --> 00:06:05.047
grows two percent but rejection with the belief cap remains near ten

00:06:04.927 --> 00:06:07.083
percent the nominal level

00:06:07.686 --> 00:06:12.033
The numerical example on the right explains the mechanism Two hundred

00:06:11.913 --> 00:06:16.595
dollars becoming two hundred forty is real growth After a fifty dollar

00:06:16.475 --> 00:06:21.827
cap fifty becoming fifty contains no growth These numbers are illustrative

00:06:22.132 --> 00:06:23.863
not simulation parameters

00:06:24.504 --> 00:06:29.379
Here the selected cap can remove the signal Controlling false positives

00:06:29.427 --> 00:06:34.250
does not guarantee detecting a positive effect on total revenue

00:06:35.270 --> 00:06:39.269
Our next step is to learn a useful belief from the experiment itself

00:06:39.574 --> 00:06:42.696
This is work in progress not a completed result

00:06:43.308 --> 00:06:48.364
First can a simple belief represent different effect shapes including

00:06:48.244 --> 00:06:50.155
growth concentrated in the tail

00:06:50.728 --> 00:06:55.951
Second can we preserve false-positive control when learning selection

00:06:56.102 --> 00:06:58.864
and testing all use the same sample

00:06:59.501 --> 00:07:04.827
Third how does belief error affect power and when does sample splitting

00:07:04.707 --> 00:07:05.188
help

00:07:05.783 --> 00:07:10.310
The current theorems assume a fixed belief They do not establish the

00:07:10.190 --> 00:07:15.258
full learned-belief pipeline or a general guarantee of power Near-tie

00:07:15.151 --> 00:07:20.155
calibration fixed-target inference assumption diagnostics and full

00:07:20.035 --> 00:07:22.784
simulation reproduction also remain open
